it might seem intuitive that we can  predict tomorrow's weather pretty well  but not whether it will rain on Tuesday  three weeks from now but we didn't  always think that way about the weather  our understanding of the atmosphere and  its patterns was forever changed by the  contributions of two exceptional  pioneers MIT professors Jule Charney and  Edward Lorenz heralds of the modern era  of meteorology Charney and Lorenz each  in his own way left an indelible mark on  MIT and the world at the height of the  second world war an unprecedented demand  for weather forecasting expertise fueled  a nationwide effort to train a  generation of meteorologists among the  young recruits was UCLA graduate student  Joule Gregory Charney who had discovered  a talent for mathematics at an early age  anticipating the United States entry  into World War two and encouraged by  Caltech professor Theodore von Karman he  signed up for a course in meteorology in  1940 seeing an opportunity to excel  meanwhile on the other side of the  country at MIT an aviation cadet and  mathematics graduate named Edward Norton  Lorenz began his own training in 1942 in  the Department of meteorology course 19  marking the beginning of a lifelong  connection to the Institute when the war  ended Lorenz returned to MIT to complete  his master's degree and PhD in  meteorology after graduating in 1948 he  stayed on as a research scientist  working with the Victor Starr  the general circulation of the  atmosphere after publishing a landmark  paper on atmospheric energy cycles  called  available potential energy and the  maintenance of the general circulation  Lorenz joined the faculty in 1955 his  advisor Victor Starr had been recruited  by Henry Houghton head of mi t--'s  Department of meteorology from 1942 to  1969 during his tenure Houghton  transformed the department into a  cutting-edge research powerhouse he  founded the training program attended by  Lorenz during the war  which grew the department more than  tenfold and oversaw the development of  weather radar later in a prescient move  he nurtured mi T's growing oceanography  program with help from the Woods Hole  Oceanographic Institution and saw it  integrated with Atmospheric Research  Houghton's vision and guidance laid a  far-reaching foundation for scientific  advance that would shape the  meteorological world for decades to come  while Lorentz was beginning his career  in meteorological research at MIT Jewell  Charney was beginning to embark on a new  adventure of his own  following the war Charney had returned  to UCLA to complete his doctorate in  1946 his PhD thesis dynamics of long  waves in a Barrow clinic westerly  current was groundbreaking work which  established the mechanism for the  emergence and evolution of weather  systems and what sets their structure  size and growth rate this was an early  example of Charney's aptitude for  applying mathematics to understanding  physical processes and my first  experience over the next two years  working first as a research scientist at  the University of Chicago with Carl  Gustav Rossby who founded the first  meteorology program at MIT in 1928 and  then as a National Research Council  fellow studying in Oslo Charney  developed his quasi geostrophic  equations which laid the foundation for  modern dynamical meteorology and  oceanography these equations not only  allowed large-scale motions of the  atmosphere to be analyzed without being  overshadowed by noisy effects at smaller  scales they could also be integrated  forward in time to predict the weather  in 1948 Charney took up an appointment  at Princeton University's Institute for  Advanced Study to work with  mathematician John von Neumann and  future MIT meteorology professor Norman  Phillips on the earliest numerical  weather prediction models their first  calculations were run on the ENIAC  computer and published in 1950 and in  the years that followed Charney's  theoretical meteorology group  demonstrated that the quasi geostrophic  equations  were able to reproduce important aspects  of the general circulation and even  predict the formation of cyclones this  resounding success won widespread  acceptance in the Meteorological  community for numerical weather  prediction the equations also became the  theoretical foundation of modern  dynamical meteorology and oceanography  permeating every aspect of the field  [Music]  building on this momentum Charney helped  to establish the joint numerical weather  prediction unit in 1954 a collaboration  between the u.s. weather bureau Air  Force and Navy  a year later he had a hand in starting  up the general circulation research  section of the weather bureau and while  this group would eventually become  NOAA's geophysical fluid dynamics  laboratory and relocate to Princeton  Charney was already considering a move  to MIT during an interview then provost  and vice president julia Stratton asked  Charney what could be done to improve  the meteorology department having always  been impressed by Ed Lorenz then still a  research scientist  Charney replied that the first thing to  do would be to promote Lorenz and his  adviser Victor Starr he also reached out  to recruit his Princeton colleague  Norman Phillips Lorenz and Phillips were  duly offered faculty positions and  Charney likewise joined MIT to direct  the atmospheric and ocean dynamics  project in 1956  charismatic and open-minded Charney had  a knack for bringing people together he  quickly established an informal seminar  series on geophysical fluid dynamics a  tradition that would continue for 22  years  attracting experts from around New  England in the fields of meteorology  oceanography geophysics and applied  mathematics during his 25 years at MIT  Charney mentored countless students and  made profound lasting impacts on the  field through groundbreaking research on  such diverse topics as the stability of  atmospheric and oceanic flows the  formation and evolution of weather  systems and hurricanes oceanic boundary  currents how large-scale planetary waves  extend up into the stratosphere the  formation of deserts and how westerly  winds can flip between banded and wavy  patterns he also continued to play a  role in the establishment of critical  weather-related initiatives including  the global atmospheric research program  an observational effort improving  numerical weather prediction one of  Charney's last contributions was a  pioneering study conducted for the  National Academy of Sciences the 1979  Charney report was one of the earliest  modern scientific assessments of global  warming he estimated that a sustained  doubling of atmospheric co2 would lead  to a surface temperature increase of  between 1.5 and 4.5 degrees centigrade a  range that has been vigorously debated  and has stood the test of time though  Charney and Lorenz shared a similar  background in mathematics and a mutual  admiration for one another the two never  worked together directly during their  time at MIT nevertheless their research  interests intersected and the  development of numerical weather  prediction facilitated by Charney's  equations carried Lorentz's research  into atmospheric dynamics forward  in 1967 he published the nature and  theory of the general circulation of the  atmosphere a treatise now considered a  classic work of scientific literature by  that time however a new exciting  research direction had begun to occupy  Lawrence's attention in 1963 after  noticing that very slightly different  initial conditions due to roundoff  errors generated by his LGP 30 computer  resulted in rapidly diverging solutions  Lorenz published a landmark article on  chaos theory the idea that nonlinear  systems such as weather are practically  unpredictable despite their  deterministic evolution the Lorenz  system three differential equations that  relate to the motions of a fluid layer  warmed from below and cooled from above  is one of the fundamental systems  studied in chaos theory these simple  equations revealed startling behavior  which led Lorenz to an important insight  though the chaotic solutions never  repeat they nevertheless tend to look  very similar even when starting from a  range of initial conditions run many  times the solutions trace out a  previously unknown mathematical object  that Lorenz termed a strange attractor  this fractal set of solutions introduced  new tools to analyze nonlinear chaotic  systems helping to answer questions like  why some weather pattern geometries are  common while others seem never to occur  continuing his study of predictability  Lorenz demonstrated in 1969 that the  nonlinear interaction among motions of  many different scales could lead to loss  of predictability even when the model is  perfect and the initial errors are  vanishingly small  [Music]  I think this is what we should have  expected all the time but I never heard  this theory was popularized as the  butterfly effect the idea that small  changes can have large consequences  lorenza's discovery not only  revolutionized to the understanding of  fluid turbulence and weather but also  led to advances in mathematics the  natural sciences and engineering Lorenz  was a vital member of MIT for over 60  years serving as the head of meteorology  from 1977 to 1981 much-beloved as an  instructor and mentor he was repeatedly  honored as best teacher of the year by  MIT graduate students and he continued  to conduct research and give lectures  well after becoming a professor emeritus  in 1987 he passed away on April 16th  2008 and three years later MIT created  the Lorenz Center in his honor dedicated  to furthering the basic understanding of  climate science by comparison Jewell  Charney's tenure at MIT was relatively  brief after a fierce battle with cancer  he died on June 16th 1981 just two years  after publishing his pioneering report  on the connection between carbon dioxide  and climate even so his impact on MIT  and around the world stands  shoulder-to-shoulder with out at his  colleague and friend Ed Lorenz born 100  years ago  both men fundamentally reshaped the  science of meteorology during their  lifetimes  Charney saw the dawn of numerical  weather prediction and its widespread  influence on nearly every aspect of  atmospheric and oceanographic research  Lorenz shifted our basic conception of  weather and climate from deterministic  phenomena to chaos and this insight  suggested new ways to examine nonlinear  chaotic systems an impact that spread  outward from meteorology to transform a  host of other disciplines but perhaps  their most enduring legacy is the  community of people trained taught and  inspired by Joule Charney and Edler  well I discovered after moving to MIT  was that I what I did enjoy mostly was  working or intensive legal aggressions  on thesis search problems because I find  that my own work is greatly enhanced and  positive times much more enjoyable when  I can today the study of atmospheric  science continues at MIT in the wider  context of eats the Department of Earth  atmospheric and Planetary Sciences  formed in 1983 from the merger of  meteorology and Earth Sciences now 100  years later the program in atmospheres  oceans and climate within eeap's  celebrates and honors the lives of  Charney and Lorenz as their students and  their students students continue to walk  in their footsteps exploring the myriad  reverberations of the work they set in  motion  [Music]  you
 

